Fixes #407.

**What was wrong.** `Presentation::alloc_id` bumped `next_id` with `saturating_add`: a presentation whose `next_id` reached `u32::MAX` — importable through `fix_next_id` from a hostile `.deckcraft` — handed that same id to every new slide, shape, layout and media. Duplicate ids make lookup, selection and references ambiguous, silently, on every later insert.

**The fix.** `alloc_id` returns `Option<u32>` via `checked_add`, and every caller refuses with a clear error naming its command (`NO_ID_SPACE`, "the presentation has used every object id and can hold no more"):

- engine: insert/duplicate/media/layout commands, `reid` (paste/duplicate re-id helper) and `add_shape` now take the caller's command id so refusals are attributed correctly; `bring_media` propagates;
- model: `new_slide` → `Option<Slide>`, `add_media` → `Option<MediaId>`; `blank_presentation`'s title slide keeps a `debug_assert` + graceful skip (a fresh deck starts at id 256 and cannot exhaust);
- pptx export: the synthesized blank layout in `sanitized()` uses `checked_add` too — the same latent mint, now guarded;
- format: `repair` re-ids duplicate slides while it can; a duplicate it cannot re-id stays as it is (the deck opens and renders; growth is refused; only that duplicate keeps its ambiguous id — `slide.delete` by id would remove both twins, so the doc comment and code say exactly that).

**Deliberately rejected:** renumbering the loaded graph — it needs cross-document reference remapping (issue #406's territory) and a wrong remap is worse than a loud refusal.

**Tests** (the audit's asked-for coverage): model — `alloc_id` at MAX returns `None` repeatedly, fresh media bytes are refused, `new_slide` is `None`; engine — with `next_id = MAX`, `slide.new`, `shape.insert`, `slide.duplicate` and `edit.duplicate` all return errors and the document is byte-identical (no id repeats); format — `repair` keeps an unre-id-able duplicate visible without inventing an id. All other former call site…

Fixes #419.

`numfmt::format` treated every digit placeholder between the first and last as one fixed-point field, so for `0.00E+00` the exponent zeros became extra decimals and the `E+` literals in the middle were dropped: 1234 showed as `1234.0000` on chart axes and data labels (`Chart::axis_label` / `Chart::data_label`).

A section with an exponent part (`E+`/`E-`/`e+`/`e-` followed by digit placeholders) now goes through a new `sci` helper:

- the placeholders before the `E` form the mantissa, formatted by the existing fixed-point code (now a `fixed` function), so the language's decimal mark still applies;
- `E+` always shows the exponent sign, `E-` only a minus; the count of `0`s after it sets the exponent's minimum width;
- a mantissa that rounds up to 10 (9.999 as `0.00E+00`) carries into the exponent (`1.00E+01`);
- several integer placeholders (`##0.0E+0`) give engineering notation, with the exponent a multiple of their count (12345 -> `12.3E+3`);
- the `e` keeps the case of the code; negative values and zero go through the same sign and section handling as other formats.

Formats without an exponent part take the unchanged fixed-point path.

Tests: `scientific_formats` covers large, small, negative and zero values, exponent widths, `E-`, rounding carry, a lowercase `e`, engineering notation and a comma-decimal language. It fails on `main` (`1234.0000` vs `1.23E+03`).

Fixes #395.

`tile_group` checked each group's `tg_start..=tg_end` range on its own, but not against the groups already received for the frame. A stream could resend a non-final group again and again (each copy appended to `job.tiles`, and later one `TileState` per entry), or skip/reorder tiles and still get the frame accepted.

AV1 requires `tg_start` to be the tile right after the previous group's `tg_end` (6.10.1). Tiles are pushed to `job.tiles` one per tile number, so `job.tiles.len()` is exactly the next expected tile. The new `check_tile_group_start` rejects any group whose `tg_start` differs, before anything is appended, with the same `Error::Invalid` used by the existing "tile group range" check. As with that check, the pending frame is dropped on error.

How each point of the issue is handled:
- duplicate coverage: rejected (`tg_start` < tiles received);
- skipped coverage: rejected (`tg_start` > tiles received);
- out-of-order coverage: rejected (either of the above);
- pending payload/state budget: with groups forced to be contiguous, a frame can never hold more than `num_tiles` tile entries, so accumulation is bounded by the frame header's tile count.

Valid streams are unaffected: a single group starts at 0 with nothing received, and each following group starts where the previous one ended.

Test: `decoder::tests::tile_groups_must_be_contiguous` covers in-order groups plus duplicate and skipped starts.

Fixes #391.

`write_file` (used by Save, Save As and the exports in `cmd/file.rs`) wrote to `"{path}.saving"` with `std::fs::write` and then renamed it over the destination. Every save of the same file shared that name, `std::fs::write` truncated whatever was already there, and an existing symlink at that path was followed.

Now `write_file`:

- names the temporary sibling `{path}.{pid}.{counter}.saving`, so two processes or two saves in one process never share it;
- opens it with `OpenOptions::create_new`, which fails on any existing file or symlink instead of opening it (it tries a new name, up to 16 times, if one is taken);
- writes through that handle, calls `sync_all`, closes it, then renames it over the destination, so the bytes are on disk before the document is marked saved;
- removes the temporary file if the write, sync or rename fails, and leaves the old destination as it was.

Tests (in `crates/engine/src/cmd/file.rs`):

- `write_file_does_not_touch_a_predictable_saving_sibling`: a file already at `deck.deckcraft.saving` keeps its contents, the destination gets the new bytes, and no temporary file is left. Without the fix the old code overwrites that file and renames it away.
- `write_file_failed_rename_keeps_destination_and_cleans_up`: the destination is a directory, so the rename fails; the save returns an error, the directory is still there and no temporary file is left. Without the fix `target.saving` stays behind.

Part of the audit described in the companion issue (findings, reachability arguments, and patches for the remaining defects are filed there).

