Pictures can now be sharpened or softened (Picture Format > Corrections), and the amount survives a PPTX round trip.

In PowerPoint, Sharpen/Soften goes from -100% (soft) to +100% (sharp). The Corrections gallery offers Soften 50%/25%, 0%, Sharpen 25%/50%, and the Format Picture pane has a Sharpness slider. The file stores it as `a14:sharpenSoften@amount` (1/1000 of a percent) inside the blip's `a14:imgLayer`. This PR adds `sharpen` to `picture.adjust`, the same five presets in the Corrections menu and a Sharpness slider next to Brightness/Contrast in the pane. Render draws it as an unsharp mask above 0 and a blend toward a blur below 0. The radius grows with the decoded size, so the picture looks the same at any zoom. PowerPoint's own kernel isn't documented, so this matches the direction and the rough strength, not the exact pixels. Happy to switch the kernel or the strength curve if you'd rather measure them against PowerPoint exports.

When PowerPoint saves a sharpened picture, the blip is the already processed image and the original (usually a `.wdp`, which DeckCraft can't decode) sits in the layer. Pictures like that are read as processed, with or without an artistic effect: the blip is drawn as is, never sharpened a second time. The original and the amount are kept and written back unchanged. Limits, chosen to never lose that original:
- on a picture PowerPoint already processed, Sharpen/Soften is disabled in the UI and `picture.adjust {sharpen}` returns an error. Reset Picture clears the other corrections but leaves the processed picture, its original and the amount alone. Artistic effects are refused on it too;
- Sharpen/Soften is also disabled on a picture with an artistic effect, and an artistic effect is refused on a sharpened picture, so DeckCraft never writes a combination PowerPoint would read differently;
- when DeckCraft saves a picture it sharpened itself, the blip stays the original, so other readers show it unsharpened;
